  <section class="hwaet-project-content">

    <h2 class="hwaet-section-title">The Film</h2>

    <p class="hwaet-series-intro">
      Set in the New Forest in 1100, <em>Earthburn</em> follows Purkiss,
      a charcoal burner living and working in the forest in the days
      immediately before the death of King William II.
    </p>

  </section>


  <section class="hwaet-project-content hwaet-series-two">

    <h2 class="hwaet-section-title">Production</h2>

    <p>
      Filmed on the Beaulieu Estate in the New Forest, <em>Earthburn</em> features
      a charcoal making method (a pit kiln) which seems not to have been attempted
      for nearly 600 years. In consultation with local craftspeople and historians we
      performed the pit burn in conjunction with the film shoot.  
    </p>
    <p>
      Our first block of shooting was completed in September 2026, with the remainder
      of the filming scheduled for October and November this year. The complex filming
      of a real, experimentally reconstructed, historical earthburn charcoal kiln was
      a fascinating logistical challenge, but was achieved by our small team without incident
      and produced a harvest of charcoal.
    </p>
    <p>
      The wood for the charcoal burn, as well as the location and permission to perform it
      were provided by the Beaulieu Estate. A portion of the charcoal produced was then
      donated to Will Sherman of <a href="https://www.medievalarrows.co.uk">Medieval Arrows</a>
      who provided us with a custom forged medieval billhook and a period-correct arrow, which 
      were used as props in the film.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our small crew was built up through a few connections from previous work, and through
      seeking out professionals and aspiring professionals in the local area. Our intention as
      a small production company is to build a regional creative team by giving younger and 
      early-career practitioners meaningful opportunities to work alongside more experienced 
      collaborators, and to carry those relationships into future Hwaet projects wherever possible. 
    </p>
    <p>
      We are keen to hear from you if you're looking to gain experience in screen production in
      any department, and you're based in the New Forest or wider Hampshire/Wilthsire/Dorset area.
